name o1-bekaa-1982
time 1982-06-09T11:00:00Z
carry units ground stores
stop_on never

# CAMPAIGN O1 — BEKAA VALLEY, 9 JUNE 1982, THE SYRIAN SIDE (Operation Mole Cricket 19).
# Spec, anchor, sources and disputes: doc/campaigns/o1-bekaa-1982.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
#
# THE QUESTION THIS CAMPAIGN ASKS IS NOT "DOES THE MiG WIN". It is: which of the doctrine levers
# FlightBox can express MOVES the outcome, by how much, and what is LEFT after the best combination of
# them. The residue is the structural part of Bekaa — the part no decision on either side removes — and
# the campaign is built so that both halves of that sentence are measurements rather than opinions.
#
# TEMPORAL HONESTY, AND ITS DIRECTION. The MiG-29 was not at Bekaa (it entered Soviet service in 1983);
# the Syrian side flew MiG-21, MiG-23 and Su-20 [T4], aircraft with "only nose and tail alert radar
# systems and no side warnings or look-up and look-down systems" [T4]. FlightBox's MiG-29 has an SPO-15
# with eight azimuth channels, a look-down-capable N019 and an IRST. THE SUBSTITUTION MAKES THE DEFENDER
# STRONGER THAN HISTORY, every mission header says so, and the consequence is asymmetric: a bad Red
# result here says something STRONGER than 1982 did, a good one says nothing about 1982 at all.
#
# THE CLOCK is 11:00 Z on the day itself — 14:00 local, the afternoon the operation opened. Only the
# sky uses it; it changes no physics. THE GROUND is `--elev const`, a 0 m datum, and every campaign
# fingerprint below is comparable only against runs whose campaign-summary.txt records `elev const`
# (doc/missions/campaign.md section 5).
#
# `carry units ground stores` — O1 DOES NOT NARROW THE CARRY, and that is the opposite of O4's choice
# for the opposite reason: a DACT sortie ends with both aircraft landing, and Bekaa ends with 82-86
# Syrian aircraft not landing [T4]. ATTRITION IS THIS CAMPAIGN'S SUBJECT, so losses are carried.
#
# WHERE THE CARRY LANDS, AND WHY IT IS ONE CHAIN AND NOT TEN. The store carry is keyed by callsign, so a
# mission that shares a callsign with its own control ALSO inherits that control's expenditure and stops
# being a control (doc/missions/campaign.md, learnt building O4). O1 has seven controlled variants and
# one attrition chain, and they cannot overlap:
#
#   sorties 01-07   THE DOCTRINE SWEEP. Seven casts, pairwise disjoint, nothing carried in or out.
#                   01 is the baseline; 02, 03, 04, 06 and 07 are 01 with exactly one line changed;
#                   05 is 04 with one line changed. Each names its control in its own header.
#   sorties 08+10   THE CHAIN. `hawk*`, `bolt*`, `eagle*`, `boeing` and the `bk*` belt appear in both,
#                   so sortie 10 flies over the belt sortie 08 left, with the aircraft it left and on
#                   the magazines it left.
#   sortie 09       the CONTROL of 08, with a wholly disjoint cast (`talon*`, `spike*`, `condor*`,
#                   `raven`, the `jm*` belt), so it neither takes from 08 nor gives to 10.
#
# THE PRICE OF THAT LAYOUT, STATED. Ten slots hold one baseline, six controlled variants, one control
# pair and one chain — and nothing else. The spec's piecemeal-versus-massed pair (its missions 8 and 9)
# is therefore NOT BUILT: a clean control pair and a carry chain cannot share a mission slot, and the
# lever that lost is the one gap C15 already calls the weakest instrument in the set ("committed
# piecemeal is expressible only as spawn times, not as a decision" — and a .fbm has no spawn times, so
# it would have been a spawn DISPLACEMENT). What bought the slot is the anchor's own decisive mechanism.
#
# THE TEN SORTIES AND THE ONE LEVER EACH CHANGES — each file's own header carries its binding reading
# rule (doc/missions/INDEX.md rule 5) and this list is the campaign's index only:
#   01 controlled     the baseline: a controller who calls, a radar that radiates, a belt on its net
#   02 no-gci         [ctrl 01] the GCI brief deleted            -> blind at spawn
#   03 gci-cut        [ctrl 01] the GCI brief truncated to call 1 -> CONFIDENTLY blind, mid-intercept
#   04 beam           [ctrl 01] the entry geometry: 45 deg, constant bearing
#   05 beam-blind     [ctrl 04] the GCI brief deleted on the geometry a controller exists for
#   06 blind          [ctrl 01] `set rwr off`: the 1982 warning receiver, bracketed by deletion
#   07 early-launch   [ctrl 01] designate at 16 nm, release at 1.4 x Rtr
#   08 belt-netted    the SEAD run against an intact, netted, cued belt        <- CHAIN HEAD
#   09 comms-jam      [ctrl 08] `set jam_comm_m` 0 -> 90000: the 707s, as one number
#   10 mole-cricket   the whole battle, every lever at its measured best       <- CHAIN TERMINUS
#
# READING RULE FOR THE CAMPAIGN. The exit code is the WORST step's and is expected to be non-zero: nine
# of the ten sorties are measuring rigs whose own headers say the verdict is the telemetry. The
# campaign's verdict is the `campaign MISSION_RESULT`, `campaign CARRY`, `campaign ATTRITION` and
# `campaign EXPENDED` lines plus the per-sortie readings, never the code.

mission ../missions/o1-01-controlled.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-02-no-gci.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-03-gci-cut.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-04-beam.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-05-beam-blind.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-06-blind.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-07-early-launch.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-08-belt-netted.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-09-comms-jam.fbm
mission ../missions/o1-10-mole-cricket.fbm
